# Producer Portal

What producers see. Different audience, same data.

---

# Producer Portal — the external surface

A separate Blazor app, dual Azure AD auth (internal + external schemes).

- **Home** — public landing; gates Sales Intake card on `IsInternalUser`
- **`/SplitIntake/{Id}`** — producer self-service of their own splits
- **`/SplitIntakeList/{All,My,Team,Submitted,Approved}`** — filtered worklist
- **`/reports/testreport`** — embedded PowerBI report
- **Hierarchy gate** — `SplitIntakePortal.CanViewOrEdit_Hierarchy` requires matching cost center

*Configuration touchpoint:* `ApplicationAccess.CanAccessProducerPortal` is the master gate. Without it, the drawer stays hidden.

---

<!-- _class: section -->

# Configuration playbook

Where to make changes — and where not to.

---

# The "safe to change" surfaces

These are configured per-tenant without code changes.

- **Statement Templates** — every carrier format
- **Match Rules** — learned rules (deactivate, don't delete)
- **Write-off Reason Codes** — lookup table seeded; add new entries via SQL
- **Accrual Configuration** — cost center inclusion + broker exclusion
- **Role / Permission / Feature Area / Access Level** — full security CRUD via UI
- **Provider Filter JSON** — per-header product scoping

*Net: 90% of "we need to change X" is in this list.*

---

# The "needs a developer" surfaces

Don't promise these in scoping. They're code changes.

- **Workflow transitions** — adding/removing status transitions
- **Permission policy names** — adding a new policy area
- **Integration handoff destinations** — adding a new AH folder
- **New entities** — anything requiring an EF migration
- **AI prompts at the system level** — the foundational prompts in `Prompts/`

*Net: 10% of changes are these. Get developer time in the SOW.*

